      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND AND OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "In two patients, one scheduled for epidural anesthesia and the other for placement of a spinal catheter for operative procedures, severe postdural puncture headache developed and was refractory to conservative therapy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "The first patient had several unintentional dural punctures, and the second underwent a planned dural puncture with an 18-gauge needle for insertion of a 20-gauge catheter. When neither patient responded to conservative therapy following development of postdural puncture headache, an infusion of adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) was given prior to consideration of epidural blood patching."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Both patients obtained complete and permanent relief from their headaches."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "A single treatment with ACTH may offer an alternative therapy in the treatment of postdural puncture headache."
        }
      ]},
